    cases=[
        dict(title="The regular expression that took down a CDN",
             story=["On 2 July 2019, Cloudflare deployed a new rule to its web application firewall across its whole network. Within minutes, CPUs handling web traffic worldwide were exhausted and customers saw 502 errors on every Cloudflare domain. The service was down for about 27 minutes.",
                    "Cloudflare's own write-up says the trigger was a poorly written regular expression that caused excessive backtracking: a pattern with several greedy `.*` parts (the page analyses `.*.*=.*`) can take enormous numbers of steps on certain inputs. The page also lists many other contributing factors, among them a CPU protection that had been removed by mistake during a refactoring, a regex engine without run-time guarantees, and a process that allowed a rule to go global without a staged rollout."],
             lesson="A regex is a program and can be slow. Prefer engines with run-time guarantees for untrusted input, test performance as well as correctness, and roll changes out gradually. In your own scripts, avoid nested greedy patterns on large inputs.",
             url="https://blog.cloudflare.com/details-of-the-cloudflare-outage-on-july-2-2019/"),
    ],
